(                               CHAPTER I) Tj T*
(                       WHAT THE STOCK EXCHANGE IS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Stock Exchange has been described as the mart of the world; as the) Tj T*
(nerve-centre of the politics and finances of nations; as the barometer) Tj T*
(of their prosperity and adversity; and so on. It has also been described) Tj T*
(as the bottomless pit of London, and as worse than all the hells.) Tj T*
(Perhaps, however, the Stock Exchange can best be defined and described) Tj T*
(as a market. Just as Smithfield is the market for meat and Covent Garden) Tj T*
(the market for flowers, fruit, and vegetables, so is the Stock Exchange) Tj T*
(the market for stocks and shares.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(These stocks and shares, as everyone knows, are, roughly speaking,) Tj T*
(sleeping partnerships. The holder of railway stock is a part-proprietor) Tj T*
(of the railway, and is entitled to his proportion of its profits; the) Tj T*
(holder of shares in a mining company is similarly part-proprietor of the) Tj T*
(mine. Even although the holders of the stocks of a nation, such as) Tj T*
(Consols, or of the debenture stocks of a company, are creditors and not) Tj T*
(proprietors, they depend, for the income which those stocks yield them,) Tj T*
(upon revenue and profits, just as does a partner in a business.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It will at once be seen that although the Stock Exchange may be defined) Tj T*
(as a mere market, the wares that are displayed and dealt in are of such) Tj T*
(importance as to entitle it to the more ambitious definitions with which) Tj T*
(it has been exalted. It is worthy of being defined as the mart of the) Tj T*
(world, because these wares represent property in every part of the) Tj T*
(world, and because the orders which are executed in this mere market) Tj T*
(emanate from all over the world. The business of the Stock Exchange is) Tj T*
(more varied and cosmopolitan than that of any other mart, except,) Tj T*
(perhaps, the Money Market. The business of the Stock Exchange may be) Tj T*
(described as the business of businesses. The institution may be defined) Tj T*
(as the nerve-centre of the politics and finances of nations, because in) Tj T*
(this mere market all that makes history is focussed and finds) Tj T*
(instantaneous expression. It is worthy of being defined as the barometer) Tj T*
(of their prosperity and adversity, for a glance at the tone of this mere) Tj T*
(market, whose wares are more mercurial than these of any other mart,) Tj T*
(suffices to indicate their condition. It may almost be said that the) Tj T*
(price of Consols is the welfare of the world expressed in one figure.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Perhaps the Stock Exchange is unworthy enough to be defined as the) Tj T*
(bottomless pit, and to be described as worse than all the hells, if we) Tj T*
(look at the mere market from the point of view of those who abuse the) Tj T*
(facilities which it offers for free dealing--but only from that point of) Tj T*
(view. Without the Stock Exchange our commercial and industrial life) Tj T*
(could never have attained its modern refinements. Indirectly this) Tj T*
ET
