(heart of the City. Its west door, the Capel Court entrance, which is the) Tj T*
(most important by tradition though not by usage, faces the eastern end) Tj T*
(of the Bank of England, and the building occupies the greater part of) Tj T*
(the triangle which has Bartholomew Lane for its base, Throgmorton Street) Tj T*
(for its north side, and Threadneedle Street and Old Broad Street for its) Tj T*
(south side, the apex of the triangle being formed by the junction of) Tj T*
(Throgmorton Street and Old Broad Street.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As to the interior, it is without form, though anything but void in) Tj T*
(business hours. There is no trace of the triangle to the insider, the) Tj T*
(boundary lines being broken where the surrounding offices abut, or where) Tj T*
(they have been swept away as opportunity offered for the extension of) Tj T*
(the Stock Exchange proper. Although the site, including the surrounding) Tj T*
(offices, occupies some 40,000 square feet, the Stock Exchange itself is) Tj T*
(only about half that in floor area. The whole design of the interior) Tj T*
(architecture, Italian in style, is marked by a good deal of solidity.) Tj T*
(The walls are for the most part covered with marble, the peculiar) Tj T*
(veining of which suggested the title, Gorgonzola Hall, by which the) Tj T*
(Stock Exchange is sometimes known. Massive pillars, also marble, abound.) Tj T*
(The floor is of teak, oak, which was formerly used, having been found of) Tj T*
(insufficient durability to stand the wear and tear of the members' feet.) Tj T*
(The feature of the interior architecture, however, is the dome, 70 feet) Tj T*
(in diameter and 100 feet high, covering the central octagonal area.) Tj T*
(Beneath the Stock Exchange proper, and extending to almost the same) Tj T*
(area, is the Settling or Checking Room, the walls of which are lined) Tj T*
(with oak panelling and glazed tiles.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To the interior of the Stock Exchange the public is not admitted, the) Tj T*
(authorities and the members themselves, aided, of course, by the) Tj T*
(janitors stationed at the doors, keeping most careful guard. Many are) Tj T*
(the stories, generally exaggerated, as to what befalls the stranger who) Tj T*
(has the temerity and skill to pass within the sacred portals.) Tj T*
(Occasionally, however, distinguished visitors are shown round, and the) Tj T*
(list includes the late King Edward VII. Only once have the Managers) Tj T*
(given permission for drawings to be made of the interior for) Tj T*
(publication, and on one occasion photographs of the interior were taken) Tj T*
(and sold for charity.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(One of the most common remarks of the clerk who sees it for the first) Tj T*
(time is, "How small a place!" The impossibility of taking in the whole) Tj T*
(of the building at a glance is responsible for the false impression so) Tj T*
(often formed by the disappointed young man newly introduced to a scene) Tj T*
(of which he has heard so much. But to take one's stand at the Bar of the) Tj T*
(House, which is just inside the door at Capel Court, is to obtain a much) Tj T*
(better view than that usually offered by the waiters on effecting the) Tj T*
(introduction of a new-comer. One is then in the Consol Market, in some) Tj T*
(respects the most important of all, and looks right down the whole) Tj T*
(length of the House from west to east. The vista is fine, if not,) Tj T*
(indeed, impressive. The massive pillars, so often criticised by the) Tj T*
(members for the loss of space they involve at their huge bases, stand) Tj T*
(out with a certain solid grandeur, but the dome of what is still called) Tj T*
(the New House, the most beautiful feature of the Stock Exchange, is lost) Tj T*
ET
