(                              CHAPTER III) Tj T*
(                      THE MEMBERS AND THEIR CLERKS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(So much for the proprietary of the Stock Exchange. Let us turn to the) Tj T*
(members, those who actually deal in the market. Their number is now) Tj T*
(about 5,000, and they are assisted by some 2,500 clerks, who also have) Tj T*
(the privilege of entering the Stock Exchange. In spite of appearances,) Tj T*
(no member of the Stock Exchange is a foreigner. Unless born a Briton, he) Tj T*
(must have resided in this country for at least seven years, and must) Tj T*
(have been naturalised for at least two years. For some years a rule was) Tj T*
(in force that before a candidate could be elected a member, he must) Tj T*
(serve at least two years' apprenticeship as a clerk in the House. This) Tj T*
(was one means by which it was attempted to limit the number of members) Tj T*
(at a time when the Managers were at their wits' end to find) Tj T*
(accommodation. The existing members, of course, welcomed the) Tj T*
(restriction, as it meant a limitation of competition; and, on the other) Tj T*
(hand, this limitation can hardly be said to have been a hardship for the) Tj T*
(public, for the number of members was by no means small, and certainly) Tj T*
(no one who desired to enter into a Stock Exchange transaction could ever) Tj T*
(fear that he would fail to find a broker. Moreover, the restriction was) Tj T*
(very desirable, inasmuch as it kept out of the Stock Exchange those) Tj T*
(adventurers who, possessing some money, or next to none, sought) Tj T*
(membership to provide facilities for gambling, just as they might seek) Tj T*
(membership of a baccarat club. Further, the business of a member of the) Tj T*
(Stock Exchange is one full of intricacy, of technicality, and of) Tj T*
(responsibility; and to enter upon it without some preliminary training,) Tj T*
(such as the couple of years' apprenticeship as a clerk affords, is to) Tj T*
(court disaster both for the adventurer and for those who may be involved) Tj T*
(in his transactions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The apprenticeship qualification has now, however, been abolished as a) Tj T*
(necessary preliminary to membership, although four years' clerkship) Tj T*
(\(with a minimum of three years in the House itself\) enables a certain) Tj T*
(limited number of clerks each year to obtain membership on special) Tj T*
(terms, the number so admissible being fixed each year by the Committee.) Tj T*
(All other candidates have to comply with another formality designed to) Tj T*
(restrict the number of members, each of them having to obtain the) Tj T*
(nomination of a member retiring in his favour or of the legal) Tj T*
(representatives of a deceased member. Moreover, each clerk admitted a) Tj T*
(member on the special terms has now to buy one Stock Exchange share, and) Tj T*
(every other candidate has to buy three shares.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It goes without saying that in the admission of members account is taken) Tj T*
(of any previous business career that they may have had. No one is) Tj T*
(eligible for admission who has been more than once bankrupt or) Tj T*
(insolvent, and if he has once been proved insolvent, or has entered into) Tj T*
(any composition with his creditors, he must before admission have paid) Tj T*
(them in full and have obtained a complete discharge.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Then every applicant for membership, before his application can be) Tj T*
(considered, must be recommended by three guarantors, reduced to two in) Tj T*
