(                               CHAPTER IV) Tj T*
(                             THE COMMITTEE) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Just as the interests of the proprietors of the Stock Exchange are in) Tj T*
(the hands of a body of Trustees and Managers, so are the interests of) Tj T*
(the members under the control of a Committee, which is called the) Tj T*
(Committee for General Purposes. The Managers provide and maintain the) Tj T*
(market-place, and thus make profit on the proprietors' capital; the) Tj T*
(Committee presides over the dealings in the market, and, of course,) Tj T*
(makes no profit in any way. It consists of thirty members, elected by) Tj T*
(ballot annually. Sometimes the election is exciting, when, for instance,) Tj T*
(the policy of the Committee on any point is questioned, but more usually) Tj T*
(it is a very formal affair. But exciting or formal, a few of the) Tj T*
(ballot-papers handed in by the members are, strange to say, always) Tj T*
(spoilt. The member of the Stock Exchange is prone to give vent to his) Tj T*
(feeling of admiration or indignation for the benefit of the scrutineers) Tj T*
(even at the expense of losing his votes altogether.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To be eligible for the office of Committeeman, one must have been a) Tj T*
(member for at least five years. The office is and always has been) Tj T*
(honorary, but now and again it is suggested that the Committee should) Tj T*
(consist, at least partly, of paid experts. Its duties are arduous, and) Tj T*
(it frequently has to settle points of the most delicate nature, whereas) Tj T*
(it is frequently complained that it does not consist of the best or most) Tj T*
(experienced members of the Stock Exchange, who do not see in the honour) Tj T*
(and dignity of the position sufficient inducement for the sacrifice of) Tj T*
(the time its duties involve. However, generally speaking, the members) Tj T*
(are held in high esteem, and seldom, indeed, does it occur that there is) Tj T*
(even an expression of disloyalty to the dictates of the Committee even) Tj T*
(on the part of those who most desire its reform.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is questionable whether throughout the whole world any body of men so) Tj T*
(constituted is endowed with more important functions or with more) Tj T*
(arbitrary power. The Committee can make practically any additions to or) Tj T*
(alterations in the code of rules, which was originally based on the deed) Tj T*
(of settlement of the Stock Exchange. Those rules have become more) Tj T*
(drastic than the law of the land. Practices which would be perfectly) Tj T*
(legal, although not very reputable, constitute a contravention of the) Tj T*
(rules of the Stock Exchange which would bring dire consequences upon any) Tj T*
(member who indulged in them. Members have been expelled the House by the) Tj T*
(Committee under the rules for an offence of which the law of the land) Tj T*
(would not take cognisance. The rules of the Stock Exchange, as) Tj T*
(administered by the Committee, are a code of honour rather than a code) Tj T*
(of law.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the course of administering this code of honour the duties of the) Tj T*
(Committee become exceedingly varied. It holds an ordinary meeting every) Tj T*
(Monday, but special meetings may be called at an hour's notice, only) Tj T*
(seven members out of the thirty being required to form a quorum in the) Tj T*
(ordinary way. Its fiat may go forth censuring, suspending, or expelling) Tj T*
(a member--expulsion, of course, meaning the loss of his profession,) Tj T*
ET
endstream
