(When the broker, armed with his client's order, goes to the market in) Tj T*
(which it can be executed, and confronts the jobber with whom he usually) Tj T*
(deals because he finds he does his business best, he asks the jobber to) Tj T*
(make him a price in the stock concerned. He does not say he wants to buy) Tj T*
(it, for that would tempt the jobber to make the price high; he does not) Tj T*
(say he wants to sell it, for that would tempt him to make it low. The) Tj T*
(jobber would not, of course, give an out-of-the-way quotation, for the) Tj T*
(broker knows the market price almost as well as he does, but he might be) Tj T*
(tempted to vary the price by a fraction. As it is, the jobber names two) Tj T*
(prices, one at which he will sell and one at which he will buy. Suppose) Tj T*
(it were the Deferred Stock of the Midland Railway Company, the jobber) Tj T*
(might quote 69-70, meaning that he would buy the stock at 69 or sell it) Tj T*
(at 70. The broker would probably think that this price was too wide and) Tj T*
(might say so, implying that the jobber ought to be prepared to buy at a) Tj T*
(higher price and sell at a lower one. There are jobbers standing near) Tj T*
(who would like the business and who would make a narrower price. At) Tj T*
(last, without much haggling, for there is little of that in the Stock) Tj T*
(Exchange, the quotation 69-3/8-69-5/8 may be obtained. The broker need) Tj T*
(not have to ask for the price at all, the jobbers may be shouting that) Tj T*
(they are willing to buy or sell at a certain price, or that they are) Tj T*
(willing to deal either way. At all events, the broker being content with) Tj T*
(the quotation 69-3/8-69-5/8, and being commissioned to buy, informs the) Tj T*
(jobber that he buys so much stock from him at 69-5/8.) Tj T*
