() Tj T*
(                              CHAPTER XIV) Tj T*
(                            A SKETCH HISTORY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Having seen what the Stock Exchange is, the work it does, and how it) Tj T*
(does it, and having considered the criticism and commentary of the Royal) Tj T*
(Commission on these points, we may with interest take a glance over the) Tj T*
(history of the Stock Exchange. For one thing, a brief sketch will) Tj T*
(indicate how the great institution has been evolved.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first Stock Exchange was in the Royal Exchange. When the National) Tj T*
(Debt was organised by William III. at the end of the seventeenth) Tj T*
(century, in such a way as to render dealing in Government securities) Tj T*
(possible--there were few other securities in which to deal--business) Tj T*
(immediately began to be transacted, and as the Royal Exchange was the) Tj T*
(place of public business resort, it was there that men met to exchange) Tj T*
(stocks, and there that the profession of stockbrokers was established.) Tj T*
(There was no organisation except such as arose from the fact that every) Tj T*
(broker, whether in merchandise or stocks, had to be licensed by the) Tj T*
(authorities of the City of London. The stockbroker was so licensed, and) Tj T*
(formed one of the groups of brokers in the Royal Exchange. There were) Tj T*
(some who regarded the new profession and its methods with little favour,) Tj T*
(and as early as 1697 an Act of Parliament was passed to regulate) Tj T*
(stockjobbing--there was no distinction between stockjobbing and) Tj T*
(stockbroking in those days. Even the mercantile brokers of the Royal) Tj T*
(Exchange objected to the new group in their midst, very noisy and) Tj T*
(rapidly growing; they even questioned its respectability, and, as a) Tj T*
(result of all this, in the year 1698 there was a great exodus of) Tj T*
(stockbrokers from the Royal Exchange.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Some remained and others transferred their place of business to South) Tj T*
(Sea House, to the offices of the East India Company and of the Hudson's) Tj T*
(Bay Company. The great bulk of them, however, made Change Alley their) Tj T*
(market-place because of its suitable open space, undisturbed by traffic,) Tj T*
(and its convenient coffee-houses. Garraway's coffee-house became a very) Tj T*
(popular resort of the stockbrokers and their clients, and, to a greater) Tj T*
(extent still, so did Jonathan's. For the first three quarters of the) Tj T*
(eighteenth century, that is, from its beginning until the year 1773,) Tj T*
(Change Alley was the Stock Exchange. It was there that, in the year) Tj T*
(1720, the scenes of the great Stock Exchange boom, panic, and collapse) Tj T*
(known as the South Sea Bubble were enacted.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the year 1733 an attempt was made to abolish speculative stock) Tj T*
(transactions altogether by the passing of Sir John Barnard's Act. It) Tj T*
(rendered it illegal to buy stock for which one did not pay, or to sell) Tj T*
(stock which one did not deliver. It outlawed mere speculative) Tj T*
(transactions for differences. That Act was not repealed until 127 years) Tj T*
(afterwards, in 1860, but at no time was it seriously operative. It) Tj T*
(spread dismay throughout the ranks of the stockbrokers at first, because) Tj T*
(a great deal of their business naturally consisted in time bargains,) Tj T*
(enabling the payment of differences instead of cash or stock down; but) Tj T*
ET
