(the proverbial coach and four was soon at hand and the Act proved a dead) Tj T*
(letter.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the course of the evolution of the profession of stockdealing, the) Tj T*
(more respectable of the brokers found themselves using Jonathan's, and) Tj T*
(by the year 1762 that coffee-house was almost regarded as the Stock) Tj T*
(Exchange, the other frequenters of Change Alley being looked upon more) Tj T*
(or less as outsiders. The aristocracy of the profession, who assembled) Tj T*
(at Jonathan's, was really the nucleus of the present Stock Exchange) Tj T*
(organisation. Amid the jealous sneers of some of their less reputable) Tj T*
(brethren, who charged them with an attempt to form an exclusive ring,) Tj T*
(they eventually, in 1773, raised a subscription, and obtained the) Tj T*
(control of a coffee-house at the north end of what are now called Royal) Tj T*
(Exchange Buildings--opposite the north-east corner of the Royal) Tj T*
(Exchange, where the Peabody statue stands. Over the door of this) Tj T*
(building was placed the inscription "The Stock Exchange." It was the) Tj T*
(first building to bear the title. But even to this building, the first) Tj T*
(Stock Exchange, admission could be obtained by the outsider at the cost) Tj T*
(of sixpence for a day ticket.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It was but a stepping-stone, however. At the beginning of the nineteenth) Tj T*
(century another subscription was raised amongst the evolutionists, this) Tj T*
(time to a capital of as much as г20,000; and the Stock Exchange was) Tj T*
(established with an exclusive membership, and with much the same) Tj T*
(constitution as exists to-day. The foundation-stone was laid in Capel) Tj T*
(Court on May 18th, 1801, and under a deed of settlement which had been) Tj T*
(drawn up, the Stock Exchange, almost as we now know it--although the) Tj T*
(deed of settlement was subject to modification in 1876 and again in) Tj T*
(1882--was soon in full swing. At the commencement there was no) Tj T*
(definitely formulated code of rules, although the unwritten laws were) Tj T*
(generally well understood and recognised by the members; it was not) Tj T*
(until 1812 that a code of rules was actually printed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Some straggling branches of the Stock Exchange profession remained) Tj T*
(outside for a time, but, in the course of years, they were practically) Tj T*
(all gathered in. For instance, a great part of the business in foreign) Tj T*
(stocks was transacted in the Royal Exchange until, in 1823, a Foreign) Tj T*
(Loan Exchange was established in a building adjoining the Stock) Tj T*
(Exchange. It had a constitution much the same as that of the Stock) Tj T*
(Exchange proper, with a Committee and so on, and it was eventually) Tj T*
(absorbed. Again, much of the business in the Funds was carried on in the) Tj T*
(Rotunda of the Bank of England from the year 1764, when the Rotunda was) Tj T*
(built--whilst the Stock Exchange was still in Change Alley--until the) Tj T*
(year 1838, when the brokers were expelled the Rotunda by the operation) Tj T*
(of a clause in the Bank Act of 1834. From that time the pre-eminence of) Tj T*
(the Stock Exchange as the market for the Funds became unrivalled, and) Tj T*
(there were some who complained that the Government in passing the Act) Tj T*
(did not, perhaps, realise that it was closing the only semblance of an) Tj T*
(open market for the transfer of the National Funds. There had been an) Tj T*
(attempt in 1810 to establish a National Fund Exchange, independent of) Tj T*
(both the Rotunda and the Stock Exchange, or failing that, to make the) Tj T*
(Stock Exchange an open market. A Bill was introduced to Parliament, but) Tj T*
