(rescinding its anti-option rule.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Two or three years later, the Stock Exchange, becoming overwhelmed with) Tj T*
(business, had little time to consider its own domestic affairs. The new) Tj T*
(company boom of 1824-5 added immensely to the scope of Stock Exchange) Tj T*
(business, and though, as is usually the case, a crash followed the boom,) Tj T*
(it left the Stock Exchange a much more important institution in the eyes) Tj T*
(of the public than it ever was before. For one thing, the newspapers) Tj T*
(began to publish a daily account of its transactions. The mania and) Tj T*
(panic of 1825 were repeated in 1835 and again in 1845, the cycles) Tj T*
(lasting just a decade. The boom and collapse of 1835 were connected with) Tj T*
(foreign loan issues, and in 1845 was the great railway mania.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the middle of the century, in 1850, the number of members of the) Tj T*
(Stock Exchange was only 864, and the annual subscription was only г10.) Tj T*
(The Official List at that time contained the names of fewer than 300) Tj T*
(securities; until 1843 it had been published not daily, but only twice a) Tj T*
(week. Soon after the middle of the century, the Stock Exchange was) Tj T*
(entirely pulled down and rebuilt. During the operation the members found) Tj T*
(a temporary home in the Hall of Commerce, which is now Parr's Bank, in) Tj T*
(Threadneedle Street. They assembled in their new building in March,) Tj T*
(1854. Much agitation arose in 1860, both inside and outside the Stock) Tj T*
(Exchange, in favour of the fixing of a uniform scale of brokers') Tj T*
(commissions. There were many meetings of members, but no more came of) Tj T*
(the agitation than has come of less serious attempts since.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The Companies Acts of 1860 and 1862, establishing the principle of) Tj T*
(limited liability, had naturally an important effect on business, and) Tj T*
(the speculation to which it gave rise aggravated the crisis of 1866, the) Tj T*
(Overend-Gurney crash. This cataclysm led to the passage of Leeman's Act) Tj T*
(designed to prevent sales of bank shares of which the seller is not) Tj T*
(possessed. The legislature recognised the distinction which exists) Tj T*
(between bank shares and all others, because of the delicate nature of) Tj T*
(banking business--depositors, seeing the shares falling, rush to) Tj T*
(withdraw their money, and thus spread ruin.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It was not until 1873 that the Stock Exchange Clearing House was) Tj T*
(established, although nowadays it seems strange that the business of the) Tj T*
(settlement could ever have been arranged without it. An attempt had been) Tj T*
(made to establish a Clearing House in 1851, but it came to nothing. Even) Tj T*
(in 1873 the Clearing House was launched under private auspices, and it) Tj T*
(was not until seven years later that it came under the official control) Tj T*
(of the Committee. The chief characteristic of the seventies, however,) Tj T*
(was the number of commissions and inquiries dealing with Stock Exchange) Tj T*
(affairs. There was the Select Committee on Foreign Loans in 1875, which) Tj T*
(made some remarkable disclosures as to the methods of borrowing by South) Tj T*
