(An important enlargement of the Stock Exchange was opened in January,) Tj T*
(1885, the addition being made of what is still called the New House.) Tj T*
(Three years before, in 1882, "Burdett's Official Intelligence," the) Tj T*
(Stock Exchange encyclopцdia, had appeared for the first time. It had a) Tj T*
(precursor in "The Railway Intelligence," and in 1899 its title was) Tj T*
(altered to "The Stock Exchange Official Intelligence," the alteration) Tj T*
(not being without significance. Soon after the New House was opened, in) Tj T*
(January, 1885, the Stock Exchange was honoured by a visit, in the month) Tj T*
(of March, from the late King Edward, then Prince of Wales.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the following year, 1886, brokers were finally freed from the control) Tj T*
(of the authorities of the City of London. From time immemorial they had) Tj T*
(had to be licensed, and although the control was for a long time real) Tj T*
(and even salutary, it had been gradually whittled away until nothing) Tj T*
(remained but the payment by the broker of an annual licence fee. Even) Tj T*
(this was abolished in 1886.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The latest great shock to credit which the country has suffered, the) Tj T*
(Baring crisis of 1890, had comparatively little effect upon the Stock) Tj T*
(Exchange. Remedies had been arranged by the Bank of England even before) Tj T*
(the difficulties were known, and Stock Exchange prices had begun to) Tj T*
(recover from the effects of the crisis within a week of the announcement) Tj T*
(of its existence. A deputation from the Stock Exchange presented an) Tj T*
(address to the Governor of the Bank of England, expressing its high) Tj T*
(appreciation of the admirable and effective manner in which the crisis) Tj T*
(had been overcome.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On the Stock Exchange practice of making a market, a couple of important) Tj T*
(judgments were delivered in 1892. In the course of one of them Mr.) Tj T*
(Justice Wright remarked that "if persons for their own purposes of) Tj T*
(speculation create an artificial price in the market by transactions) Tj T*
(which are not real, but are made at a nominal premium merely for the) Tj T*
(purpose of inducing the public to take shares, they are guilty of as) Tj T*
(gross a fraud as has ever been committed and of a fraud that can be) Tj T*
(criminally brought home to them.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In 1895, during a notable boom in South African mining shares, there) Tj T*
(occurred what was known as the Battle of Throgmorton Street--between) Tj T*
(members of the Stock Exchange and the police. Members had to appear day) Tj T*
(after day on a charge of obstruction before the magistrate, who) Tj T*
(suggested that when the Stock Exchange was closed they might, instead of) Tj T*
(obstructing the thoroughfare, arrange to use the Royal Exchange. Far) Tj T*
(more serious strife, again closely connected with South African affairs,) Tj T*
(arose in the closing days of the year, when the Jameson Raid into Boer) Tj T*
(territory was followed by a severe slump in prices. The political) Tj T*
(tension of which this raid was an outcome ultimately developed into the) Tj T*
(Transvaal War, which resulted in a long period of Stock Exchange) Tj T*
(stagnation and depression. In 1896, and again in 1897, the 2-3/4 per) Tj T*
