(                               CHAPTER XV) Tj T*
(                             A BROKER'S DAY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Having mastered roughly some of the mysteries and technicalities of the) Tj T*
(Stock Exchange, we are now able to follow a broker through an average) Tj T*
(day of his life with some interest. The broker's office-boy sighs) Tj T*
(wearily to himself when he hears clerks in other walks of life dilating) Tj T*
(upon the easy hours enjoyed by the Stock Exchange and its employees. It) Tj T*
(is true that the office-boy does not have to reach his sphere of labour) Tj T*
(early enough to sweep out the place and dust the desks; that is all done) Tj T*
(for him; but he is supposed to be on the spot by about half-past nine.) Tj T*
(Until ten he is pretty well lord of the office, for the Stock Exchange) Tj T*
(clerks, as a rule, have a very easy time as regards hours. They need) Tj T*
(rarely get to town much before ten o'clock, except the youngest of the) Tj T*
(juniors, and the first personage of importance to arrive is he upon whom) Tj T*
(falls the duty of glancing through the correspondence. Of course, it all) Tj T*
(depends upon the size of the office, just as it does in other) Tj T*
(businesses, how this branch of work is organised. In the case of a big) Tj T*
(firm the letters are carefully sorted, and each department has its) Tj T*
(proportion handed over, one dealing with transfers, another with) Tj T*
(dividends, and so forth. But a smaller office may be more representative) Tj T*
(of the general run of Stock Exchange work, and in such a one the) Tj T*
(correspondence comes into the scope of the chief clerk if the partners) Tj T*
(are not energetic enough to deal with it themselves.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
