<< /Length 3665 >>
stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(is, the cause of ruin to those who abuse its facilities--to those who) Tj T*
(are gamblers rather than either investors or speculators. It is quite) Tj T*
(easy to draw the line between the three classes of business, or rather,) Tj T*
(the two classes of business and the one class of folly. The investor) Tj T*
(lends his money on perfectly safe security to the aid of commerce and) Tj T*
(industry, expecting a comparatively small return, but that a safe one.) Tj T*
(By so doing he is encouraged in thrift, whilst commerce and industry) Tj T*
(enjoys the benefit of the capital, and the Stock Exchange is the medium) Tj T*
(of it all. The speculator, less conservative, risks money, the loss of) Tj T*
(which he is perfectly well able to afford, in the furtherance of) Tj T*
(experiments in commerce and industry, be it the trial of a patent or the) Tj T*
(opening up of a mine. He expects a big return should the experiment) Tj T*
(prove successful, but is prepared to face the loss should it turn out) Tj T*
(otherwise. He hopes to enrich himself, and without his aid commerce and) Tj T*
(industry would make none of those rapid strides which are for the) Tj T*
(welfare of the world, for speculation is the handmaid of enterprise. The) Tj T*
(gambler, frequently usurping the name of speculator and thus bringing) Tj T*
(legitimate speculation into ill-odour, risks money which he cannot) Tj T*
(afford to lose, staking all in an inordinate desire for riches. Although) Tj T*
(the line between speculation, which is perfectly legitimate, and mere) Tj T*
(gambling can easily be drawn, it must be drawn by each individual for) Tj T*
(himself. A perfectly legitimate speculation for a rich man might be an) Tj T*
(act of folly for a man of moderate means. Between risking money which) Tj T*
(can easily be afforded in a project well studied and thought out,) Tj T*
(however uncertain, and risking money which cannot be afforded in a) Tj T*
(project of which nothing is known, on the chance that some extraneous) Tj T*
(circumstance may arise to multiply its value, there is a wide gulf) Tj T*
(fixed. The gamblers it is from whom are drawn the operators and dabblers) Tj T*
(who play with the Stock Exchange as they would with a pack of cards, or) Tj T*
(with the chances of the race-course, to their own loss and to the profit) Tj T*
(of insiders who, whenever they are called upon to play such a game,) Tj T*
(always show in the long-run that they know most of it. It is the Stock) Tj T*
(Exchange gambler rather than the investor, or the speculator who moves) Tj T*
(legitimately within his means, who may rejoice in the boom with its) Tj T*
(roaring business and rising prices, but who forms the real element of) Tj T*
(danger in a slump with its sudden depreciation, and who frequently turns) Tj T*
(the slump into a crisis, and eventually into a panic. These panics are) Tj T*
(less frequently with us than they were, less widespread and less) Tj T*
(disastrous in their effects, thanks to the rapid dissemination of true) Tj T*
(news, and to the highly organised state to which the machinery of) Tj T*
(finance has attained; but they will ever recur to provide a wholesome) Tj T*
(check to those who are tempted to go beyond their depth, allowing) Tj T*
(legitimate speculation to deteriorate into mere gambling. The Stock) Tj T*
(Exchange offers facilities for such gambling, just as food offers) Tj T*
(facilities for over-eating; yet food is not only a good thing, but a) Tj T*
(necessity, and so is the Stock Exchange.) Tj T*
